<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>ar</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>FaceType AI</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(MARKETING_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<!--
	  UILaunchScreen, not UILaunchStoryboardName. This target has no
	  LaunchScreen.storyboard and never had one, so the old key named a
	  resource that does not exist. iOS treats an app whose launch screen
	  cannot be resolved as one that does not support the device's native
	  resolution: it runs the app letterboxed in a scaled compatibility
	  window, which on a modern iPhone means the whole React Native root view
	  renders at the wrong size. An empty UILaunchScreen dictionary is the
	  documented way to ask for the plain system launch screen with no
	  resource file at all, and it is available on every OS this target
	  supports (deployment target 15.1; the key is iOS 14+).
	-->
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict/>
	<!--
	  arm64, not armv7. The deployment target is iOS 15.1 and no armv7 device
	  ever ran iOS 11, so declaring a 32-bit requirement described a device set
	  that cannot exist and risks an App Store Connect validation error against
	  an arm64-only binary.

	  `arkit` is deliberately NOT required (client app v1). The client camera
	  is AVFoundation + MediaPipe and runs on any front camera, TrueDepth or
	  not. The ARKit field-study lane checks availability at runtime instead:
	  FaceTypeCapture.isAvailable/start and FaceTypePreviewView all gate on
	  ARFaceTrackingConfiguration.isSupported and fail closed.
	-->
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
	</array>
	<key>UIViewControllerBasedStatusBarAppearance</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  Camera is required: the client app photographs the face automatically
	  (frames of the analysed video stream), keeps the accepted photos
	  encrypted on the device and uploads them to the user's own account; the
	  field-study lane measures face geometry. The string has to say that
	  photos are kept and uploaded; the previous wording ("no photo is
	  uploaded and no frame is kept") is false for the client app.

	  Note what is absent. There is no NSFaceIDUsageDescription, because this app
	  never calls LocalAuthentication and must never be able to. Declaring the
	  key would grant a capability FT-00 §3 forbids.
	-->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>يستخدم التطبيق الكاميرا الأمامية لتصوير وجهك تلقائيًا وقياس ملامحه. تُحفظ الصور المقبولة مشفّرة على جهازك ثم تُرفع إلى حسابك وفق موافقتك. لا يستخدم التطبيق Face ID.</string>
	<!--
	  iOS 17 reaction effects (hand-gesture balloons, confetti...) are drawn
	  into the camera frames themselves. Their gestures start disabled for this
	  app; the client camera also refuses to store any frame while a reaction,
	  Portrait or Studio Light effect is active (`effects_active`).
	-->
	<key>NSCameraReactionEffectGesturesEnabledDefault</key>
	<false/>
	<!--
	  iOS 14 and later gate every connection to an address on the local
	  network behind this permission. A development build reaches both the
	  Metro bundler and the backend over a LAN address (that is exactly what
	  `.xcode.env` documents setting FACETYPE_API_URL to), so without this
	  key a build installed on a real iPhone cannot be shown the permission
	  prompt and is refused the connection. Release builds talk to a remote
	  HTTPS origin and never trigger it.

	  The wording has to say what actually happens. An earlier version of this
	  string promised that no face data reaches another device on the network,
	  which is the opposite of the truth for the case the permission exists to
	  cover: the whole point of pointing FACETYPE_API_URL at a LAN address is
	  that the scan's geometry is uploaded to that machine. A consent string
	  that under-states what is sent is worse than no string at all (Codex
	  review 2026-08-26T13-14-11Z, item 4).
	-->
	<key>NSLocalNetworkUsageDescription</key>
	<string>أثناء الاختبار المحلي فقط، يتصل التطبيق بخادم التطوير على شبكتك المحلية ويُرسل إليه صور الوجه الملتقطة وبيانات هندسة الوجه الناتجة عن المسح. لا يحدث هذا في الإصدار المنشور، الذي يتصل بخادم آمن عبر الإنترنت.</string>
	<key>CFBundleLocalizations</key>
	<array>
		<string>ar</string>
		<string>en</string>
	</array>
	<!--
	  Google Sign-In returns to the app through its reversed iOS client id
	  (com.googleusercontent.apps.NNN). The value comes from the build setting
	  FACETYPE_GOOGLE_IOS_URL_SCHEME, declared empty in ios/project.yml until
	  the Google iOS OAuth client exists; the owner fills it once, there or as
	  an xcodebuild override. While it is empty the Google provider stays
	  unconfigured (client app v1: provider not configured, never a fake
	  success) and a distribution build must not be submitted.
	-->
	<key>CFBundleURLTypes</key>
	<array>
		<dict>
			<key>CFBundleURLName</key>
			<string>google-sign-in</string>
			<key>CFBundleURLSchemes</key>
			<array>
				<string>$(FACETYPE_GOOGLE_IOS_URL_SCHEME)</string>
			</array>
		</dict>
	</array>
	<!--
	  The app's cryptography is the operating system's own: HTTPS from its
	  networking stack, and (client camera spool) AES-GCM and SHA-256 from
	  Apple's CryptoKit, used only to protect the user's own photos at rest on
	  the device. It implements no encryption algorithm of its own. Declaring
	  that here is what lets a TestFlight/App Store Connect upload complete
	  instead of stopping on the export-compliance question every time a build
	  is submitted. Whether OS-provided at-rest encryption keeps this `false`
	  is an export-compliance determination the account holder must confirm
	  before distribution.
	-->
	<key>ITSAppUsesNonExemptEncryption</key>
	<false/>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<key>NSAllowsArbitraryLoads</key>
		<false/>
		<key>NSAllowsLocalNetworking</key>
		<true/>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
